pci_walk_bus(bridge->bus, pci_configure_extended_tags, NULL);
```
Reason:
Certain Broadcom devices (HT1100 and HT2000) do not handle 8-bit Tags correctly as Completers, which violates PCIe specifications. When Extended Tags are enabled, these devices fail to process requests properly, leading to system hangs and errors such as GPU lockups and unresponsive keyboards.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
DECLARE_PCI_FIXUP_EARLY(PCI_VENDOR_ID_SERVERWORKS, 0x0140, quirk_no_ext_tags);
DECLARE_PCI_FIXUP_EARLY(PCI_VENDOR_ID_SERVERWORKS, 0x0141, quirk_no_ext_tags);
DECLARE_PCI_FIXUP_EARLY(PCI_VENDOR_ID_SERVERWORKS, 0x0420, quirk_no_ext_tags);
DECLARE_PCI_FIXUP_EARLY(PCI_VENDOR_ID_SERVERWORKS, 0x0422, quirk_no_ext_tags);
```
Reason:
These statements add quirks for the affected Broadcom devices, disabling Extended Tags for them. This ensures that the devices do not attempt to use 8-bit Tags, thus preventing the issues with peer-to-peer DMA and allowing the system to function correctly without GPU lockups or unresponsive peripherals.

